  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable conditions with lower humidity\nearly this week.\n\nA weak and small jet streak in the upper levels, along with a\nvery weak disturbance rotating into the region from the north\nmay just be enough dynamics to keep a shower in spots over the\nChesapeake region and southern Maryland through daybreak. \nNothing severe, but just a shower in spots. Otherwise, high\npressure will build into the region through Tuesday. Lower\nhumidity, dry conditions and sunshine with light winds will be\nexpected during this period. The high moves to the western\nAtlantic just offshore of the East Coast Tuesday night. This\nwill allow for a south to southwesterly flow to develop and\ncontinue through Wednesday. Warmth and humidity will begin to\nincrease once again. High temperatures today will be in the low\n80s, accompanied by overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s. High\ntemperatures Tuesday should reach the middle 80s, then into the\nupper 80s by Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower & thunderstorm chances return midweek.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to slide offshore on Wednesday. \nTemperatures & humidity increase somewhat during this time, with \ntemperatures reaching the upper 80s and lower 90s by Thursday. \nMeanwhile, an upper-level trough centered over the Hudson Bay will \nproduce a stemming cold front that tracks across the East Coast \nsometime mid-to-late week. Current guidance has widespread \nprecipitation potential pinpointed for Thursday afternoon and \nevening as this frontal boundary tracks through. The 18z guidance \nremains a bit uncertain regarding exact timing for this, as the \nECMWF is starting to slide later in the week. Regardless, showers & \nthunderstorms could occur as this front slides offshore towards the \nend of the week. The severe threat continues to look marginal right \nnow , but some model guidance is starting to show a possibility for \na couple of stronger storms given the increased heat & humidity at \nthis point combined with potential wind shear. \n\nTemperatures drop again in the wake of the front late in the week, \nwith conditions becoming much drier looking towards the weekend as \nhigh pressure moves in overhead.",
